 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>The lore of Marchesa Luisa Casati has outlived her. The bon vivant walked her pet cheetah on a diamond leash, wrapped a live boa constrictor around her neck as jewelry, and owned a flock of albino blackbirds that she dyed different colors to match her party themes.</p><p>The “bewitching eccentric” of early-20th-century Venice is someone who Bibhu Mohapatra has often turned to for a jumping-off point, but especially recently while navigating grief and loss in his personal life. “She believed that all her life was a work of art—and every day she started a new canvas,” the designer said backstage postshow at The Pierre Hotel on the Upper East Side.</p><p>For spring 2027, Mohapatra dialed up the saturation with a palette that Casati would have gladly cosigned. To kick things off, a candy floss pink ensemble in cady set the tone—both in terms of vibrancy and optimism—while its feathered hem nodded to the Indian designer’s penchant for more-is-more dressing right now. “I wanted something to boost me,” he said.</p><p>Among the sequins and trains, marigold, another hue indicative of positivity, as well as a flower that tends to symbolize new beginnings, stood out for its simplicity and effortless beauty, particularly when used for a sheer, romantic poet blouse and asymmetric duchesse satin skirt. Beaded moths on every model’s stilettos ensured there was plenty of razzle-dazzle no matter what. “I actually love moths,” Mohapatra said, laughing. “They’re prophetic, so when you see one, it means something positive.”</p><p>Pretty dresses and skirts rendered in four-way printed Chantilly lace also brought levity and lightness and showed the designer’s mastery of the fabric. While denim was likely not in Casati’s repertoire, Mohapatra’s well-heeled clientele is evidently leaning more into it. This season, he’s offering them a floor-sweeping trench with epaulets, a sturdy shirtdress with a dramatic collar, and slouchy indigo-wash jeans to team with their black-tie finery.</p></div>",
